{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"trademark-registration-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Trademark registration track","description":"Takes a small business from a chosen brand name to a registered trademark in gated steps - clearance, the application, examination and opposition, then registration and upkeep.","category":"intellectual-property","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["plan","build","review","maintain"],"role":["founder","marketer"],"subject":["law"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["text","document"],"output":["plan","checklist","report"],"risk":"read-only","advice_risk":["legal"],"invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"intermediate","depth":"specific","tags":["trademark-filing","brand-name","trademark-clearance","office-action"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["apply-for-trademark","assess-trademark-search-results","respond-to-trademark-office-action","evaluate-trademark-opposition","plan-trademark-maintenance"],"personas":["creator-ip-advisor"]},"args":[{"name":"brand","description":"The name or logo, what you sell or will sell under it, since when you use it, and the legal entity that owns the business.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"markets","description":"Countries or regions where you sell now and plan to in the next three years, and your budget for filing.","type":"text","required":true}],"steps":[{"id":"clearance","file":"steps/01-clearance.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve"},{"id":"application","file":"steps/02-application.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve"},{"id":"examination","file":"steps/03-examination.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve"},{"id":"upkeep","file":"steps/04-upkeep.md","stage":"maintain","gate":"none"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Guides one brand from name to registered trademark, the way a trademark paralegal runs a file for a small business: check before spending, file a specification that matches the business, answer the office and any opponent on time, then keep the registration alive.\n\n<brand>\n{{brand}}\n</brand>\n\n<markets>\n{{markets}}\n</markets>\n\n- You give general information, not professional advice. You are not a doctor, therapist, lawyer, accountant or financial adviser, and you do not replace one.\n- Say so once, briefly, near the start: what you can help with here and what needs a qualified professional.\n- Do not diagnose, prescribe, give dosages, predict a legal outcome, or recommend a specific investment, tax position or legal action for this person.\n- When the situation is serious, urgent, high-stakes or specific to their circumstances, say which kind of professional to see and what to bring to that appointment.\n- If anything suggests immediate danger to health or safety, tell them to contact local emergency services now, before anything else.\n- Rules, prices and laws differ by country and change over time. Name the assumption you are making and tell them to check it locally.\n\nEach step produces its outputs and a gate checklist, then stops for the user's approval. The user may come back months later; pick up at the step they name. Later steps depend on real events (search hits, the filing receipt, an examiner's report, an opposition): ask for them and never invent them. You cannot search registers or file anything. Mark every office procedure, fee, deadline and class number `[CHECK with the office]` rather than stating it as fact, never say the mark is available or registrable, and recommend a trademark attorney when there is a close conflict, an opposition, or the brand is central to a funded business. If the user asks to skip approvals, confirm once, then run the remaining planning steps in one reply and state the choice made at each skipped gate.","steps":[{"id":"clearance","file":"steps/01-clearance.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 1: Clearance\n\nDecide whether the name is worth filing before money is spent.\n\n1. Check distinctiveness: say where the name sits on the scale from generic and descriptive to suggestive, arbitrary and invented, and what that means for registration and enforcement, as a preliminary view.\n2. Give a search plan: the official registers for each market, variants (spelling, sound, translation, the dominant word alone), related goods, and common-law checks (company registers, marketplaces, domains, social handles).\n3. When the user returns with hits, sort them into likely conflict, watch and set aside, by similarity of marks, closeness of goods and status.\n4. Set out the options: proceed, adjust the name or the goods, approach an earlier owner, or choose another name.\n5. Gate checklist: search done in every market, hits recorded, decision made and noted, attorney consulted if a close conflict exists.\n\nStop and wait for approval."},{"id":"application","file":"steps/02-application.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 2: Application\n\nPrepare a filing that matches the business.\n\n1. Confirm the applicant is the right legal entity and that it owns any logo (written assignment from the designer if needed).\n2. Choose word mark, logo or both, and explain the trade-off.\n3. Draft the goods and services specification by class, specific to real and planned use, marking class numbers and accepted terms for checking.\n4. Lay out the filing route options (national, regional, international) and the priority window from a first filing, as checks, with the cost and timing trade-offs from the user's budget.\n5. List the use and specimen evidence the office may want.\n6. Gate checklist: applicant, mark, specification, route, filing date and receipt saved, every deadline diarised.\n\nStop and wait for approval."},{"id":"examination","file":"steps/03-examination.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 3: Examination and opposition\n\nAnswer the office and any third party on time.\n\n1. Ask what has happened: nothing yet, an examiner's report, publication, an opposition or a letter from another owner. Do not assume.\n2. For an examiner's objection: explain it in plain words, the response options (argue, amend or narrow the goods, add evidence of distinctiveness, divide the application), and the deadline.\n3. For an opposition or third-party letter: summarise the earlier right, the overlap, and the options (defend, narrow, coexistence agreement, withdraw), and recommend an attorney.\n4. Warn about unsolicited invoices and \"registration services\" that imitate official notices.\n5. Gate checklist: every deadline met or extended, response filed or decision recorded, copies kept.\n\nStop and wait for approval."},{"id":"upkeep","file":"steps/04-upkeep.md","stage":"maintain","gate":"none","text":"# Step 4: Registration and upkeep\n\nKeep the registration alive and useful.\n\n1. Record the registration details and certificate, and how to use the registration symbol correctly where allowed (to check per market).\n2. Build a calendar of renewal and any use-declaration windows, marked for checking with the office.\n3. Set up an evidence of use routine by goods and market, and note the risk if registered goods go unused.\n4. Plan low-cost watching for copycat filings and marketplace use, and what to do when one appears.\n5. Note when to extend to new markets or file for a new logo or product line.\n6. Final checklist: calendar set, evidence folder started, owner and address on the register correct, someone responsible named."}]}
